 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Boundary Trimming:** If the original resolution is not an exact multiple of the window size, voxels at the boundary must be discarded to maintain regular grid structure [S1, unofficial]. This results in a slight loss of data at the edges.
-- **Precision:** Averaging many voxels can lead to floating-point precision issues if the values are large or numerous. Use double precision if necessary.
-- **Anisotropic Resolution:** The provided method [S1, unofficial] uses a single `window_size`. If the resolution reduction is anisotropic (e.g., $N_x$ reduces by 2, $N_y$ by 4), the window size must be calculated per axis. The sources do not explicitly detail anisotropic window calculation, so this implementation assumes isotropic or uniform windowing as per the primary note.
+- **Boundary Trimming:** Voxels at the boundary that do not fit into a complete window of size `window_size` are discarded to maintain the regular grid structure [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** Averaging over windows may introduce floating-point accumulation errors for large volumes; double precision is recommended if values are large [G].
+- **Anisotropic Resolution:** The source notation implies a uniform or scalar window size [S1, unofficial]. Per-axis window sizes are not detailed in the sources.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Image Slicing & Downsampling
-Extracts 2D slices from 3D volumes for terminal plotting and downsamples them. It iterates over two axes excluding `along_axis`, setting the third axis to a specific `slice` index, mapping 3D indices to 2D coordinates. For downsampling, it computes a stride `voxels / new_size`. For each new cell, it samples the function $f$ at `unroll(i) * stride`. Alternatively, it accumulates scaled values into target cells: $g(\{cell\_x, cell\_y\}) += f(\{i, j\}) \times scale$, where $scale = new\_size[0] / f.size(0)$ [S2, unofficial].
+### [Image Slicing & Downsampling](19590-image-slicing-downsampling.md)
+This method extracts 2D planar slices from 3D volumetric data and reduces spatial resolution to enable visualization on systems with limited memory or rendering capabilities. It serves as a lightweight alternative to full 3D volume rendering, allowing for the inspection of scalar fields and RGBA modalities on commodity hardware. Unlike the parent method [3D Average Pooling for Coarsening](13934-3d-average-pooling-for-coarsening.md), which coarsens the entire 3D dataset, this approach operates on 2D projections or subsets, significantly reducing the data volume for terminal plotting or 2D visualization tools.
+
+### [Volume Binning](22553-volume-binning.md)
+Volume Binning is a coarsening method that partitions a 3D voxel grid into uniform $N \times N \times N$ blocks and replaces each block with the arithmetic mean of its constituent values. It serves as a specific implementation of 3D average pooling for multiresolution level-of-detail generation, distinct from general adaptive downsampling by enforcing strict spatial uniformity in the bin structure. Unlike [Image Slicing & Downsampling](19590-image-slicing-downsampling.md), which may operate on 2D projections or variable strides, Volume Binning operates natively in 3D data space.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the original volume, as each voxel is visited once per axis pass.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the input and output volumes. Intermediate reshaped arrays may require temporary memory proportional to the input size.
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; preserves average intensity; computationally efficient.
-- **Limitations:** Loses high-frequency details; boundary trimming discards edge data; assumes isotropic downsampling in the primary description [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When average intensity is the key metric (e.g., extinction, density) and performance is critical.
-- **When not to use:** When preserving maximum values (use [VoxelGrid Down Sample](1014-voxelgrid-down-sample.md)) or when exact boundary preservation is required without trimming.
